## ABSTRACT
Hydrocarbons separation in petrochemical industries is a key, energy-consuming stage in the manufacture of high-quality added-value products—hence the need for more efficient materials and environmentally friendly methodologies to improve this process. In this context, we have studied the effect of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) pore functionalization in hexane isomers separation, isolating the robust isoreticular zinc(II) bipyrazolates Zn(BPZ), showing no pore decoration, $Z n({\mathsf{M e}}_{2}{\mathsf{B P}}Z)$ , the pores of which are decorated with apolar methyl groups, and $Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B P}Z(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2})$ , the spacers of which possess polar Lewis-basic functions $({\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B P Z}}=1H,1^{\prime}H-4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyrazole; ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{e}}_{2}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{P}}Z=3,3^{\prime}$ -dimethyl- $1H,1^{\prime}H{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyrazole; $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P Z}(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2}=3,5\cdot$ -diamino$1H,1^{\prime}H{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyrazole; DMF $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dimethylformamide). After characterizing $Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B P}Z(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2})$ as per its crystal structure and thermal behaviour, and all the three MOFs as per their textural properties, we investigated, from the experimental and computational points of view, the impact of the square one-dimensional channels decoration on the separation of the hexane isomers, demonstrating the relevance of pore constrictions in the resolution of the title alkanes mixture.
## KEYWORDS
adsorption, molecular sieving, nitrogen-donor ligands, gas separation, powder diffraction, variable-temperature pulse gas chromatography
## Introduction
Hydrocarbons separation in petrochemical industries is a crucial, energy-consuming step toward the manufacture of high-quality added-value products such as gasoline, polymers, plastics [1]. As a representative example, the efficiency of internal combustion engines is related to the reactivity of the hydrocarbons comprised in gasoline. An indirect assessment of gasoline grade is the so-called research octane number (RON) [2]. Linear hydrocarbons, reacting faster than branched ones, possess low RON. As far as the five $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{14}}}$ isomers $\mathrm{(HEX=}$ n-hexane, $2{\mathrm{MP}}=2$ -methylpentane, $3\mathrm{MP}=3$ -methylpentane, $2,2\mathrm{DMB}=2,2$ -dimethylbutane, $2,3\mathrm{DMB}=2,3$ -dimethylbutane) are concerned [3], the RON increases from the value of 30 of HEX to those of 74 and 75 of the monobranched 2MP and 3MP, respectively, and those of 94 and 105 of the dibranched 2,2DMB and 2,3DMB, respectively. For commercial purposes, a mixture containing only $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{14}}}$ dibranched isomers is thus more appealing than one containing also the monobranched and linear isomers, due to its higher final RON.
On a large scale, after catalytic isomerization reactions, $C_{6}$ isomers are typically separated by fractional distillation, an expensive process limited by the similar physico-chemical properties of the alkanes to be separated (e.g. boiling points in the range 323–341 K). Separation by selective adsorption is a promising alternative. Presently, low RON paraffines are separated from high RON ones through the Total Isomerization Process $(\mathrm{TIP}^{\mathrm{rw}})$ from UOP [1, 4, 5], or the Ipsorb™ process from Axens [6, 7]. These processes recur to zeolite 5A, which is capable of separating linear hydrocarbons from mono- and dibranched ones, granting a final RON of 86. A better separation performance can be obtained by combining zeolite 5A with zeolite $\upbeta$ in a dual-layer pressure swing adsorption bed [8], yielding a RON enhancement up to 92. Nonetheless, due to their similar polarizability and inert behaviour, an efficient separation of mono- from dibranched hexane isomers with selectivity and capacity higher than those of zeolite 5A is still a challenge.
In view of their intrinsic porosity, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are ideal materials for adsorption and separation [9–13]. Along with tuning the nature and length of its skeleton, functionalizing the ligand has revealed a powerful tool for the optimization of MOFs properties [14].
In comparison to other functional applications, hexane isomers separation with MOFs is a less developed field [3, 15–30]. Though showing a comparable van der Waals volume $(\sim113\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})$ [31], the $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{14}}$ isomers possess different kinetical diameters (4.3, 5.0, 5.6 and $6.2\mathring\mathrm{~A~}$ for HEX, 3MP, 2,3DMB and 2,2DMB, respectively) [31]. Hence, an efficient way to separate them appears the usage of shape-controlled adsorbents [32]. With MOFs, shape-control has been achieved either through a specific pore shape or pore overall architecture, as is the case of the triangular channels of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{BDP})_{2}$ [3] $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDP}=1,4$ -bis(4’- pyrazolyl)benzene) or the inter-connected one-dimensional (1D) channels of $\mathrm{Zn(BDC)(Dabco)}_{0.5}$ [15] $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}=1,4$ -dicarboxylic acid; Dabco $^{=1,4}$ -diazabicyclo[2,2,2]octane), respectively, or through a proper functionalization of the spacers, as with the rigid tagged UiO-66 materials [24] or with the flexible MOF508 [18] and MIL-53(Fe)- $(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}$ [25] compounds.
In this work, we have studied the effect of pore functionalization in alkane separation on a series of robust isoreticular zinc(II) bipyrazolate MOFs possessing 1D square channels (Fig. 1). With this aim, $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ [33] (Zn(BPZ)DMF; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}=1H,1^{\prime}H{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyrazole, Scheme 1) showing no pore decoration, the dimethylated $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ counterpart [34] $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ})$ ·DMF; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}=3,3^{\prime}$ -dimethyl- $1H,1^{\prime}H{-}4,4^{\prime}.$ - bipyrazole, Scheme 1), the pores of which are decorated with apolar methyl tags, and the novel diamino-tagged $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(Zn(BPZ(NH_{2})_{2})}$ )·DMF; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}=3,5$ -diamino- $1H,1^{\prime}H\cdot$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyrazole, Scheme 1; $\mathrm{DMF}=$ dimethylformamide), the spacer of which bears polar Lewis-basic functions, have been synthesised and characterised. In a second step, the impact of their pore decoration on the separation of the hexane isomers has been experimentally and computationally studied, showing the relevance of pore constrictions on the mixture resolution.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1  Synthesis and preliminary analytical characterization
Zn-BPZ∙DMF [33], Zn-Me2BPZ∙DMF [34] and Zn$\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ ∙DMF were synthetized with a good yield $(75\%-83\%)$ following conventional synthetic paths. In all the cases IR spectroscopy [36] (Fig. S1 in the ESM) revealed the presence of clathrated solvent, as highlighted by the bands centred in the range $1,657{-}1,658\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , which can be ascribed to the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{v(C=O)}$ stretching mode of the carbonyl group in DMF.
## 2.2  Crystal structure analysis
$\mathrm{Zn}{\mathrm{-BPZ}}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}{\mathrm{.DMI}}$ is isoreticular [37] to Zn-BPZ [33] and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ [34]. For the sake of completeness, its crystal and molecular structure will be briefly described in the following. $\mathrm{Zn{\mathrm{-BPZ}}(N H_{2})_{2}}\cdot$ DMF crystallizes in the tetragonal space group $P4_{2}/m m c$ and shows a three-dimensional (3D) porous network characterized by square 1D channels parallel to the [001] crystallographic direction (Fig. 1(a)), where tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ nodes (Fig. 1(b); see the caption to the figure for the main bond distances and angles at the metal ions) are connected by exo-tetradentate ligands. The topological analysis of the crystal lattice [38] revealed a sra network type. At ambient conditions, $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ possesses an empty volume of $\sim43.8\%$ [39], which translates into a pore volume per cell of $\sim0.32\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1<1>}$ , values which are comparable to those of Zn-BPZ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ (Table 1). The channel dimensions of the studied ${\mathrm{MOFs}}^{<2>}$ $(5.3\mathrm{-}7.2\mathring\mathrm{A}$ , Table 1) lay in the same range of the kinetic diameters of the $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{14}}}$ isomers (kinetic diameters $=4.3\substack{-6.2\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ [31]). Consequently, it is expected that a strategic molecular sieving effect will take place.
## 2.3  Thermal behaviour
As shown by the investigation of the thermal behaviour (Fig. S2 in the ESM), $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ possesses a good thermal stability both in air and under nitrogen $\left(T_{\mathrm{dec}}\sim723\ \mathrm{K}\right)$ , comparable to those of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ $T_{\mathrm{dec}}\sim723\:\mathrm{K}$ [33] and $\sim733\mathrm{~K~}$ [34], respectively). The observed high thermal stability is a very important feature in order to allow adsorption/ separation processes at temperatures close to $500\mathrm{K}$ (see below). Loss of clathrated solvent (Fig. S2(a) in the ESM) is denounced, below $610~\mathrm{K}.$ , by a two-step weight loss overall amounting to $\sim24\%$ (corresponding to $\mathrm{1~mol}$ of DMF per formula unit; calculated $\mathrm{loss}=24.3\%$ ). Neither loss of crystallinity nor phase transition occurs during the desolvation process, as assessed by in situ variable-temperature powder X-ray diffraction (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM). As highlighted by a Le Bail parametric treatment of the data (Fig. S3(c) in the ESM), the decrease of the $a\mathrm{-}$ and $c$ -axis upon solvent loss leads to a unit cell volume shrinkage of $\sim-2.8\%$ in the temperature range 303–613 K. Like $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ [33], $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ [34] and other $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ bipyrazolate MOFs [40], also $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ is relatively stiff, an occurrence which can be beneficial for molecular sieving capability enhancement [41]. Above $703\mathrm{K},\mathrm{Zn}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{BP}\mathrm{Z}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ starts losing crystallinity.
## 2.4 $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption
The pore accessibility of $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ was probed by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption at $77~\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. S5(a) in the ESM). For the sake of completeness, the textural properties of the actual batches of the other two MOFs were unveiled too (Figs. S5(b) and S5(c) in the ESM). While $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ shows a type I adsorption isotherm [42], $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ possess a type IV adsorption isotherm with a small hysteresis loop. The low Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas are in the range $123{-}572\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Table S1 in the ESM), comparable to previous results on different batches [33, 34], the highest value being that of the untagged MOF. As retrieved from the $t$ -plot analysis, the micropore area of $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ is equal to $\sim50\%$ of the total pore area. The Horvath-Kawazoe differential pore volume plot, obtained applying a cylinder pore geometry, shows a sharper peak at $\sim7.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . The mesopore component can be related to interparticle voids.
Also $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at 273 and $298\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. S4 and Table S1 in the ESM) is indicative of the limited accessibility of the porous structure upon ligand functionalization, with an observed decrease in adsorption capacity following the trend $Z\mathrm{n-BPZ>}$ $\mathrm{Zn-Me_{2}B P Z>Z n{\cdot}B P Z(N H_{2})_{2}}$ suggesting that the effect of the steric hindrance of the substituents on the ligands prevails on the preferential interactions generated with the adsorbate.
## 2.5  Hexane isomers separation
Table S4 in the ESM collects the $C_{6}$ partition coefficients of representative MOFs reported in the literature up to date: to the best of our knowledge, only $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{O})](6\mathrm{FDCA})_{3}$ outperforms $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ in terms of separation, yet at $303\mathrm{K}$ and $10\mathrm{kPa}$ [45]. At similar temperature and pressure conditions, $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ show definitely better selectivity, also in comparison to zeolites $\upbeta$ [46] and MOR [47, 48] (Table S4 in the ESM).
The thermodynamic information of the hexane isomers adsorption process in the pores of $\mathrm{Zn{\mathrm{-}B P Z}(N H_{2})_{2}}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ - ${\bf M e}_{2}{\bf B P Z}$ was extracted from the van’t Hoff plots of $\ln(V_{\mathrm{N}})$ vs. $1/T$ (Fig. 2(d) and Fig. S5(d) in the ESM) at $463\mathrm{K}.$ The results are gathered in Table 2. The adsorption enthalpies $(-\Delta H_{\mathrm{iso}},$ Table 2), follow the same trend of the partition coefficients (and are roughly directly proportional to them, Figs. 3(b) and $3(\mathrm{c})_{.}^{\cdot}$ ), suggesting that the separation is enthalpy driven. Overall, the isosteric heat of adsorption values lie in the range $-85.0/-71.9$ and $-61.7/-56.9\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn-Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{BPZ}}.$ , respectively, i.e. are comparable to or higher than those typically shown by zeolites [46], which is an expected occurrence with small-pore adsorbents [21]. On the other hand, the adsorption entropy $\left(-\Delta S_{\mathrm{diff}},\right.$ Table 2) is roughly directly proportional to the partition coefficients in the case of $\mathrm{Zn{\mathrm{-}B P Z}(N H_{2})_{2}}$ (Fig. 3(c)). As for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn-Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{BPZ}}.$ , it is $\mathrm{~\sim~}150~\mathrm{J{\cdot}K^{-1}{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ for all the alkanes apart from 2,2DMB, for which it is $\sim10\mathrm{~J}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ higher. Finally, the Gibbs free energy shows an exponential or a logarithmic behaviour as a function of the partition coefficients for $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ or $\begin{array}{r}{Z\mathrm{n-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ},}\end{array}$ respectively (Figs. 3(b) and 3(c)). All in all, these results are indicative of the dependence of the adsorption equilibrium on the hexane isomers shape.
## 2.6  Molecular simulations
The $C_{6}$ isomers preferential adsorption sites of the three MOFs were individuated using the software Materials Studio [49]. The results are indicative that all the isomers are located in the centre of the 1D channels and share no preferred interactions with the metal centres or the pore walls (Fig. 4 and Figs. S6 and S7 in the ESM). Nonetheless, the host–guest C···C distances (Table S5 in the ESM) are in the ranges 3.4–3.8, 3.2–3.6 and $3.2\substack{-3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ, $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}{\cdot}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{P}Z$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}.$ respectively. The shortest interactions in $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ are slightly longer than those in the other two MOFs, which agrees with the higher bulk hindrance imposed by the Me and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ moieties. This occurrence may concur to explain the easier diffusion of the hexane isomers inside the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ porous structure (see also below), hence the irreversible alkane incorporation into this MOF. These features differ from the situation found by Finsy et al. [21] in MIL-47, which shows a higher offset of the linear HEX isomer from the pore channel centre in comparison with the branched isomers. This difference among MIL-47 and the title MOFs can be traced to the larger pore width in MIL-47 [21] vs. the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -bipyrazolate systems (8.5 Å vs. 5 Å).
We have also determined the diffusivity coefficients of the $C_{6}$ isomers for the Zn-BPZ, $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ systems at $463\mathrm{~K~}$ (Table 3) by means of the molecular dynamics tools of Materials Studio. The diffusivity coefficients are of the order of $10^{-4}{-}10^{-5}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ for $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ and in the $10^{-5}–10^{-7}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ range for $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ . The higher values of the diffusivity coefficients for $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ in comparison to those of $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ agree with two different scenarios: i) highly accessible pores in $Z_{\mathrm{{n-BPZ}}}$ and ii) more constricted pores in $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\mathrm{-BPZ}}({\mathrm{NH}}_{2})_{2}$ . The higher values for the diffusivity coefficients of the $C_{6}$ isomers in $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ suggest that all the guest molecules diffuse inside its inner particle pore structure, explaining their irreversible trapping inside (see also above). For $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn{\cdot}B P Z(N H_{2})_{2}},$ the lower values for the diffusivity coefficients of the $C_{6}$ isomers point out a hindered diffusion inside their pore structure; concomitant, weaker interactions at the external particle surface are more plausible. Moreover, the higher diffusivity of linear and monobranched isomers in comparison with dibranched ones agrees with the experimental separation results. This occurrence might explain why $n$ -hexane is able to diffuse inside and outside the porous system, while the branched (bulkier) isomers are only able to interact with the outer pore structure of the $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ particles.
## 3 Experimental section
## 3.1  Materials and methods
All the chemicals and reagents employed were purchased from commercial suppliers and were used as received without further purification. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ and $\mathrm{{H_{2}B P Z}}$ were synthesized according to previously published procedures (Sharko et al. [50] and Boldog et al. [51], respectively). IR spectra were recorded as neat from 4,000 to $600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with a PerkinElmer Spectrum One System instrument. Elemental analyses $(\mathrm{C\%})$ , $\mathrm{H\%}$ , $\mathrm{N\%}$ ) were performed with a Fisons Instruments 1108 CHNS-O elemental analyser. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectrum of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ(NH}_{2}\right)_{2}$ ligand was acquired at room temperature with a Bruker Avance 400 instrument working at $400~\mathrm{{mHz}}$ and using deuterated dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO- $\mathbf{d}_{6}$ ) as solvent. The simultaneous thermal analysis (simultaneous thermogravimetric analysis and differential scanning calorimetry) on $\mathrm{Zn}{\mathrm{-BPZ}}({\mathrm{NH}}_{2})_{2}$ was carried out under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ flow $(40~\mathrm{\mL{\cdot}m i n^{-1}})$ with a PerkinElmer STA 6000 thermal analyser in the temperature range $^{303-1,173\mathrm{K}}$ and with a heating rate of $10\mathrm{K}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) qualitative analyses were carried out with a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer (see the section describing the structure characterization for the instrument specifics), acquiring data at room temperature in the $4^{\circ}-35^{\circ}$ $2\theta$ range, with steps of $0.02^{\circ}$ and time per step of 1 s (see Fig. S8 in the ESM for representative examples). The nature and purity of all the batches employed for the functional characterization were assessed by elemental analysis, IR spectroscopy and PXRD.
