Microcrystalline powders of $\mathrm{Zn\mathrm{-BPZ(NH_{2})_{2}\mathrm{{\cdotDMF}}}}$ were deposited in the hollow of a silicon zero-background plate $0.2\ \mathrm{mm}$ deep (Assing $\mathrm{srl}$ , Monterotondo, Italy). Data acquisitions were performed on a vertical-scan Bruker AXS D8 Advance θ:θ diffractometer, equipped with a Lynxeye linear position-sensitive detector, an X-ray tube $\mathrm{TuK}\alpha$ , $\overset{\cdot}{\lambda}=1.5418\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}$ ), a filter of nickel in the diffracted beam and the following optical components: primary beam Soller slits $(2.5^{\circ})$ , fixed divergence slit $(0.5^{\circ})$ , antiscatter slit $(8~\mathrm{mm})$ . The generator was set at $40\ \mathrm{kV}$ and $40~\mathrm{mA}$ . After a preliminary acquisition for qualitative analysis $(2\theta\:=\:5^{\circ}-35^{\circ}),$ ), a diffraction data set for a full structure characterization was collected in the range $2\theta=5^{\circ}-105^{\circ}$ , with steps of $0.02^{\circ}$ and time per step of $10~\mathsf{s}.$ . A comparison of the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Zn}{\mathrm{-BPZ}}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}{\mathrm{.DMF}}$ to that of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ [33] suggested that they are isostructural. Nonetheless, an independent indexing procedure was performed. A standard peak search followed by profile fitting was used to estimate the low-tomedium-angle peak maximum positions, providing approximate unit cell parameters through the singular value decomposition algorithm [53], as implemented in TOPAS-Academic V6 [54]. The space group was assigned based on the observed systematic absences. The structure refinement was carried out with the Rietveld method, as implemented in TOPAS-Academic V6, starting from the crystal structure of Zn-BPZ [33]. The crystallographically independent portion of the ligand was modelled by a rigid body through the $\textbf{z}$ -matrix formalism; on account of the difficulty in localizing hydrogen atoms via X-ray diffraction, the amino groups were fixed coplanar to the ligand plane. To locate the solvent, structure solution was then performed by a combined Monte Carlo/simulated annealing approach with TOPAS-Academic V6. DMF was described as a rigid body<1> and its position of the centre of mass and orientation were let to vary. Average values were initially assigned to bond distances and angles for both the ligand<2> and solvent<1>. During the final Rietveld refinement stages, bond distances (except for the $\mathrm{C-H}$ distances) were refined in a restrained range of values<3>. A Chebyshev polynomial function was successfully used for the background description. A refined, isotropic thermal parameter was assigned to the metal atoms; lighter atoms were given an isotropic thermal parameter $2.0\mathrm{\AA}^{2}$ higher. The fundamental parameters approach [55] was used to model the peak profile. Two tan(θ)-dependent spherical harmonics were convoluted to a Lorentzian function in order to describe the different anisotropic peak broadening of the sharper $[h0l]$ Bragg reflections with respect to the $[h k l]$ ones; a Gaussian contribution further lowered the $R_{\mathrm{wp}}$ figure of merit. The final Rietveld refinement plot is shown in Fig. S12 in the ESM. The pertinent CIF file is supplied as the ESM.
