## 3.4  Variable‐temperature powder X‐ray diffraction
As a complement to the simultaneous thermal analysis, the thermal behaviour of $\mathrm{Zn{\cdot}B P Z(N H_{2})_{2}}.$ DMF was investigated in situ by variable-temperature powder X-ray diffraction<4>. Using a custom-made sample heater (Officina Elettrotecnica di Tenno, Ponte Arche, Italy), $20\mathrm{mg}$ of as-synthesized $\mathrm{Zn\mathrm{-BPZ(NH_{2})_{2}\mathrm{\cdotDMF}}}$ was heated in air from $303\mathrm{~K~}$ up to decomposition, with steps of $20~\mathrm{K}$ a PXRD pattern was acquired at each step, covering the $2\theta$ range $8^{\circ}-31^{\circ}$ . Le Bail parametric refinements of the data acquired before loss of crystallinity enabled us to disclose the behaviour of the unit cell parameters as a function of the temperature.
## 3.5 $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ adsorption
The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ with a Micromeritics Tristar 3,000 volumetric instrument under continuous adsorption conditions, using a liquid $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ bath with $99.999\%$ purity $\Nu_{2}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were measured at 273 and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ using the same instrument as above under continuous adsorption conditions. In all cases, the samples were activated at $403\mathrm{~K~}$ under high vacuum $10^{-6}$ Torr) for $^\textrm{\scriptsize8h}$ prior to measurement. To identify the pressure range of the $\Nu_{2}$ isotherm where to apply the BET model, we applied the consistency criteria described by Rouquerol [56].
## 3.6  Variable‐temperature pulse gas chromatography
Gas-phase adsorption at zero coverage surface was studied by the pulse chromatographic technique, using the oven of a gas chromatograph equipped with a stainless-steel column (length $\sim15\ \mathrm{cm}$ , $\mathcal{O}_{\mathrm{int}}=0.4~\mathrm{cm};$ ) packed with $\sim400~\mathrm{mg}$ of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BPZ, $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ or $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ . The detection of the eluted cromatographic peaks was carried out employing a Quadrupole Omnistar Mass Spectrometer using $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ( $2\ \mathrm{uma})$ as reference to determine the dead volume of the column (Hexane mass $86\mathrm{{uma}},$ ). Prior to each measurement, the purity and crystallinity of the samples were checked via PXRD (see Fig. S8 in the ESM). The samples were activated overnight at $443\mathrm{~K~}$ in a flow of He $(20\ \mathrm{\mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ ). Reactivation was performed after each measurement. The PXRD data collected on the samples after the experiment are reported in Fig. S8 in the ESM. The $\mathrm{{C}_{6}\mathrm{{H}_{14}}}$ isomers were injected separately in a flow of He ${\bf\dot{\tau}}_{40\mathrm{~mL}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}},$ ) in order to unveil their retention time. The zero-coverage thermodynamic parameters of the adsorption process were calculated using a van’t Hoff type analysis employing the isothermal chromatographic measurements [57]. The volume expansion of the gas entering the capillary at the different temperatures was taken into account and, consequently, the retention volumes were corrected according to
