The αHEX/alkane partition coefficients were calculated as the ratio of the $V_{\mathrm{{N}}}$ of each gas. In the case of $Z_{\mathrm{{n-BPZ}}}$ , it was not possible to evaluate the pertinent thermodynamic parameters, since this MOF traps all the alkanes within its pores in the range of temperatures studied (373–473 K).
## 3.7 Computational modelling
## 3.7.1  Adsorbate location
In order to localize the position of the $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{14}}$ isomers within the MOFs pores, the adsorption locator module of the Materials
Studio 6.0 software [49] was employed to perform Monte Carlo simulations. The used forcefield was COMPASS [59], the charge was forcefield assigned and the summation methods were groupand atom-based. The simulations yielded the most stable conformation of the $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{14}}$ isomers within the pores of the three studied MOFs.
## 3.7.2  Adsorbate diffusivity
Computational modelling of the diffusivity of the hexane isomers HEX, 3MP, 2,3DMB and 2,2DMB in the pores of the three MOFs was carried out with the BIOVIA Materials Studio 2018 [49] Forcite module by running molecular dynamics simulations and determining the mean square displacement of the alkane molecules in each host, to calculate the selfdiffusivity coefficients. The crystal structure was fixed to the ambient-conditions structure determination. First, one adsorbate molecule was located with the BIOVIA Materials Studio Forcite adsorption locator module. Then, a molecular dynamics simulation was carried out at a constant temperature of $463~\mathrm{K}$ and a 50 ps time interval to track the trajectory of the guest molecule, mimicking the experimental conditions of the pulse gas chromatography experiments. Finally, a molecular dynamics simulation was performed at constant energy to track the trajectory of the guest molecule in the host unit cell and to calculate its mean square displacement.
## 4 Conclusions
We have studied the effect of pore functionalization in hexane isomers separation on a series of robust isoreticular zinc(II) bipyrazolate frameworks possessing square 1D channels. The similar size of channels and kinetic diameters of the hexane isomers leads to an important impact of pore decoration on the adsorption process. Indeed, experimental and computational studies show that the non-constricted diffusion of all hexane isomers in non-decorated Zn-BPZ leads to an irreversible trapping of all the hexane isomers in its pore structure, which makes it useless for this separation process. By contrast, the pores decoration with bulky residues in $\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{BPZ}(\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Zn-Me}_{2}\mathrm{BPZ}$ leads to a constricted diffusion of $C_{6}$ isomers inside their pore structure; concomitant, weaker interactions at the external particle surface are more plausible. Moreover, the temperature at which the separation process takes place is relevant to the conditions of the industrial isomerization used to enhance the RON value of the resolved mixture [3].
